Closes #105186

## What Problem This Solves

Fixes an issue where users trying to pass untrusted source code or tab-separated data containing tabs and newlines into the prompt engine would experience completely broken formatting and destroyed indentation layout because `sanitizeForPromptLiteral` destructively stripped tabs and newlines.

## Why This Change Was Made

The sanitization regex was configured to strip all Unicode control characters (`Cc` category), which includes tab (`\t`) and newline (`\n`). We added a negative lookahead `(?![\t\n])` to the regex in `src/agents/sanitize-for-prompt.ts` to preserve these critical whitespace characters for code structure and layout.

## User Impact

Code formatting, tabs, and newlines are preserved when code blocks are sanitized and embedded into LLM prompts, ensuring that code indentation is maintained and prompts remain highly readable for the models.

## Evidence

Unit tests in `src/agents/sanitize-for-prompt.test.ts` were updated to assert that tabs and newlines are correctly preserved, while other control characters (CR, NUL) are still stripped:
```typescript
  it("strips ASCII control chars (CR/NUL) but preserves tabs and newlines", () => {
    expect(sanitizeForPromptLiteral("/tmp/a\nb\rc\x00d\te")).toBe("/tmp/a\nbcd\te");
  });

  it("preserves tabs and newlines for code fidelity", () => {
    expect(sanitizeForPromptLiteral("line1\nline2\tindented")).toBe("line1\nline2\tindented");
  });
```
All checks run successfully on Vitest.

Closes #105188

## What Problem This Solves

Resolves a problem where the session reaper cron job catches a persistence error (e.g., EACCES, disk full) and immediately enters an unbounded retry loop. Because the throttle timestamp was never updated on error, the reaper would instantly retry on every subsequent timer tick, causing severe CPU and disk I/O thrashing.

## Why This Change Was Made

The throttle update was placed at the end of the success path. In `src/cron/session-reaper.ts`, we wrapped this update in a `finally` block so that the throttle timestamp is always updated regardless of success or failure. This ensures the standard cooldown period is respected even during errors.

## User Impact

Prevents system instability, high CPU usage, and disk I/O exhaustion in environments with temporary disk or permission issues.

## Evidence

The existing test file `src/cron/service.session-reaper-in-finally.test.ts` validates that the reaper runs its cleanup hooks safely. The change moves the throttle setting to the `finally` block inside `sweepCronRunSessions`:
```typescript
  } catch (err) {
    params.log.warn({ err: String(err) }, "cron-reaper: failed to sweep session store");
    return { swept: false, pruned: 0 };
  } finally {
    lastSweepAtMsByStore.set(storePath, now);
  }
```
Checked locally via Vitest to confirm everything runs and passes.

## What Problem This Solves

Fixes an issue where Copilot BYOK users could have the local provider proxy buffer an unbounded request body before forwarding when the SDK sent an unexpectedly large POST. Also fixes a correctness defect where chunked requests that overflowed the limit (or timed out) were torn down with `ECONNRESET` instead of receiving a deterministic 413 HTTP response.

## Why This Change Was Made

The BYOK proxy now applies the same bounded request-body guard pattern used by other plugin HTTP ingress paths. The `readRequestBodyWithLimit` shared helper previously destroyed the request socket before throwing on limit violations, which prevented callers from delivering a proper HTTP error response for chunked bodies. A new `destroyOnError` option (default `true`, backward-compatible) lets the BYOK proxy keep the socket alive for the response, then destroy afterward.

## User Impact

Users keep the same BYOK provider behavior for normal SDK requests. Declared-length and chunked overflow now both return proper 413 responses instead of silent `ECONNRESET`.

## Evidence

- Declared-length overflow returns 413 (existing gate, unchanged).
- Chunked overflow returns 413 (new streaming-reader path, previously `ECONNRESET`).
- Normal requests pass the body-size guard and reach the upstream fetch.

```text
$ npx tsx byok-proof.ts
declared-length-overflow status=413
declared-length-overflow body=Payload too large
chunked-overflow status=413
chunked-overflow body=Payload too large
normal-path reached-guard=status=502
```

<details>
<summary>byok-proof.ts</summary>

```ts
import { createCopilotByokProxy } from "./extensions/copilot/src/byok-proxy.js";
import { resolveCopilotProvider } from "./extensions/copilot/src/provider-bridge.js";

async function main() {
  const resolvedProvider = resolveCopilotProvider({
    model: {
      provider: "custom-proxy",
      api: "openai-responses",
      id: "proxy-model",
      baseUrl: "https://proxy.example/v1",
    },
  });
  const proxy = await createCopilotByokProxy(resolvedProvider);
  if (!proxy) throw new Error("proxy creation failed");

  try {
    // --- Declared-length overflow (existing gate) ---
    const r1 = await fetch(`${proxy.provider.provider?.baseUrl}/responses`, {
      method: "POST",
      body: "x".repeat(16 * 1024 * 1024 + 1),
    });
    console.log(`declared-length-overflow status=${r1.status}`);
    console.log(`declared-length-overflow body=${await r1.text()}`);

    // --- Chunked overflow (streaming reader gate, the fix) ---
    const r2 = await fetch(`${proxy.provider.provider?.baseUrl}/responses`, {
      method: "POST",
      headers: { "content-type": "application/octet-stream" },
      body: new ReadableStream({
        start(controller) {
          const totalBytes = 16 * 1024 * 1024 + 1;
          let sent = 0;
          while (sent < totalBytes) {
            const size = Math.min(1024 * 1024, totalBytes - sent);
            controller.enqueue(new Uint8Array(size));
            sent += size;
          }
          controller.close();
        },
      }),
      duplex: "half",
    } as RequestInit);
    console.log(`chunked-overflow status=${r2.status}`);
    console.log(`chunked-overflow body=${await r2.text()}`);

    // --- Normal forwarding (sanity) ---
    const r3 = await fetch(`${proxy.provider.provider?.baseUrl}/responses`, {
      method: "POST",
      headers: { "content-type": "application/json" },
      body: JSON.stringify({ model: "proxy-model", prompt: "hello" }),
    }).catch((err) => err);
    console.log(`normal-path reached-guard=${r3 instanceof Error ? "yes" : `status=${(r3 as Response).status}`}`);
  } finally {
    await proxy.close();
  }
}

main().catch((err) => { console.error("PROOF FAILED:", err); process.exit(1); });
```

</details>

